<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexandre Dumas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1802">1802</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Alexandre Dumas père, Alexandre Davy de la Pailleterie</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexandre Dumas, also known as Alexandre Dumas père, was a French novelist and playwright born in Villers-Cotterêts. His father, General Thomas-Alexandre Dumas, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> to a French nobleman and Marie-Cessette Dumas, an enslaved woman. Dumas began his career writing plays and became one of the most widely read French authors with historical novels of adventure first published as serials, including The Count of Monte Cristo and The Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musketeers</a>. In the 1840s he founded the Théâtre Historique in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He died on 5 December 1870 and was buried at his birthplace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas.jpg" alt="Alexandre Dumas" width="330" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadar_-_Alexander_Dumas_père_(1802-1870)_-_Google_Art_Project_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In France, Chateaubriand, Lamartine, Hugo, <b>Alexandre Dumas</b> and George Sand led in literature, and Delacroix and Berlioz in painting and music.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The word began as a term of disapproval: “féminisme” first appeared in an 1871 French medical thesis on men with tuberculosis who had developed feminine traits, and in 1872 <b>Alexandre Dumas</b> fils coined “féministe” for men who supported women’s rights.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The book had a major influence on the literary community and is directly referenced in 19th-century French works such as <b>Alexandre Dumas</b>'s The Three Musketeers and Edmond Rostand's Cyrano de Bergerac.</blockquote><a href="/work/don-quixote">Don Quixote · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
